# Feature completeness — the schema is a requirements document

Incident (2026-09-07): conference-registration oversold golf slots to −20.
The `available_contestants` counter existed, was labeled "Available Golfers" in
Conference Manager, and was decremented per Golfer sale by the webhook. The
agent building the new golfer sales paths even wrote, two weeks before the
breach, that "`available_contestants` decrements only for ticket name
'Golfer'" — then shipped three new sales paths on top of it with no floor, no
pre-charge check, and no warning. The user never typed "enforce the maximum"
because the counter's name already said it. A junior developer who sees
`available_contestants` just knows to incorporate it. That is the bar.

## The binding principle (everything below is illustration, not a whitelist)

Mistakes SIMILAR IN NATURE to this incident must not recur — not merely this
exact one. The user should never need to be surgically precise for intent to
be honored. If a reasonable developer reading the code, schema, labels, helper
text, or admin UI would infer an expectation, that expectation is a
requirement. Absence of a pattern from the examples in this rule is never
license to ignore it; when in doubt, implement it or surface it — silently
skipping is the only wrong answer.

## Read the anatomy before extending (mandatory)

When creating or extending any flow, first read the Strapi content-type
schema(s), webhooks, and services for every entity the feature touches, and
derive requirements from the fields themselves — the prompt is not the full
spec, the system is:

- For EACH field ask: who writes it, who reads it, and what behavior does its
  name imply? Examples (not exhaustive): `*_available` / `available_*` /
  `purchasable_*` / `maximum_*` → stop selling at 0. `*_price` → must be
  charged. `registration_start/end` → gate access. Status enums → every state
  needs handling. Anything counted, dated, priced, flagged, or labeled in an
  admin UI implies behavior somewhere — find it or flag it.
- Any field your new code paths write, decrement, consume, or bypass is IN
  SCOPE for your change, even if no prompt mentions it.
- If you discover such a field is unenforced, either enforce it in the paths
  you are adding or state unprompted in your summary: "NOTE: nothing enforces
  `<field>` — writes continue past its limit." Never observe an inventory
  counter in writing and then build sales paths that ignore it.

## Enforcement standards

- Server-side is authoritative: re-check capacity/gating at write time in
  Strapi and reject violations; use atomic decrements. Client checks lose
  races and are UX only (show remaining / sold-out, block adding, surface
  server rejections with the shared red toast).
- Enforce in EVERY entry point: online, kiosk (`source=kiosk`), admin
  override, resubmit, and direct API. One path unenforced = not implemented.
- Count with the same logic the manager dashboards display, so "available"
  can never go negative there.

## Before calling it done

- Summaries must include a requirement → enforcement-location → verification
  mapping, covering both stated requirements AND schema-derived ones. Any
  schema-derived requirement intentionally skipped must be listed as such.
- E2E-verify the boundary itself (cap−1, cap, cap+1 locally), not just the
  happy path.
